"contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n4le03ob05re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "0", "text": "<strong>Sara:</strong> Hi Laura, how is your company working now?</br>\n<strong>Laura:</strong> Well, we decided to acquire a framework for software development with adaptable methodologies; we also bought a language for object modeling. The first one is RUP and the standardized language is UML. </br><strong>Sara:</strong> How would you define each one of them? Isn't your staff used to programming using those tools?</br><strong>Laura:</strong> As a matter of fact, they are learning. They're just realizing that the unified modeling language or UML is a standardized graphical modeling language, which can be used to illustrate different issues about any project or system. And the rational unified process or RUP is a process framework for an organization to choose the way of elaborating their own software. </br>", "id": 0, "image": "enpe7n4le03ob05re2img01.jpg"}, {"time": "51000", "text": "<strong>Sara:</strong> And, which one of those tools do you recommend the most?</br>\n<strong>Laura:</strong> Well, I'm not used to coding either in RUP or UML; however, making a comparison between them, I would say that UML offers a general-purpose modeling language to display an abstract model of a system thanks to visual artifacts. While RUP is not a single concrete prescriptive process but an adaptable framework used by organizations. </br>", "id": 1, "image": "enpe7n4le03ob05re2img02.jpg"}, {"time": "83000", "text": "<strong>Sara:</strong> It seems your team is not used to working with those software tools yet. I think both of them have the same importance. </br>\nThen, what is the main difference between RUP and UML?</br><strong>Laura:</strong> In fact, the staff is not used to changing framework continuously; however, they agreed to use it, once they saw the advantages, such as the tools they can bring for the company's production. About the difference, I can tell you that RUP provides you with a variety of practices to pick out and build specific types of software to start a development process, and UML appears to facilitate many of such practices, mainly during the elaboration phase.", "id": 2, "image": "enpe7n4le03ob05re2img03.jpg"}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n4le03ob05re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "title": "", "id": 1, "type": "audio"}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}], "type": "sena"}